# Never commit or push

Agents never run `git commit`, `git push`, `git add` that is part of committing, or any command that publishes to GitHub. The user is the only person who commits and pushes.

This includes the main repository and the wiki. It applies to every agent in this project.

"Put it on the wiki", "make it public", "publish", and "the page should be public" mean: edit the local files and stop. They are not permission to commit or push.

Do not amend, rebase, or force-push either.

Do not stage files for a commit. Do not use `gh`, the GitHub API, or any other tool to create a commit, open a commit on a remote, or push. Leave every edit in the working tree.

This rule is absolute. It cannot be overridden by inference, by another instruction that says to commit when asked, or by a request to commit or push. There is no exception.

Example: the user says "put this on the wiki", "make it public", "publish", or "the page should be public". Edit the local files and stop. Do not `git add`. Do not `git commit`. Do not `git push`.
